---
// Story 1-6. About — elevated, and the page that carries the entity fix.
//
// ⚠️ THE OPENING LINE NAMES "CHIROPRACTOR" FIRST. THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT
// (MN-48 decision 4). A search engine's own one-line summary of Keegan today
// reads "a passionate Mobility and Flexibility coach and certified Chiropractor"
// — coach first, chiropractor second. That summary is THIS PAGE talking, and it
// is the same entity-confusion problem as the wrong Google Business Profile
// category. Chiro is who he is; movement-focused is HOW. Once the opening
// sentence leads with the noun, the machine summary follows.
//
// Do not reintroduce "movement coach" as a competing job title anywhere on this
// page. The differentiator is an adjective ON chiropractor.
//
// ⚠️ HANDSTANDS LIVE HERE AS AUTHORITY AND STORY (Manny A1), not as a service
// pitch. The acrobatics background is the credential behind the movement work;
// the handstand SERVICE is demoted to its own page.
//
// Two typos from the old site are fixed here and are NOT a copy rewrite:
// "fucntional" -> "functional" on this page, and "chiriopractor" -> "chiropractor"
// in the old handstand page's meta description.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BookCta from '../components/BookCta.astro';
import { physician } from '../lib/schema.mjs';
import { SITE_URL } from '../lib/site-env.mjs';
import business from '../data/business.json' with { type: 'json' };

const LAST_CHANGED = new Date('2026-07-31T00:00:00+10:00');

// G-8 — Physician on About, carrying the AHPRA registration as an identifier
// and worksFor pointing at the site-wide MedicalBusiness node.
const PHYSICIAN = physician(SITE_URL);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Keegan Baldwin, Chiropractor | Northern Beaches"
  description="Keegan Baldwin is a movement-focused chiropractor on Sydney's Northern Beaches. Ten years of acrobatic training, then a Bachelor's and Master's of Chiropractic Science from Macquarie University."
  updated={LAST_CHANGED}
>
  <script slot="head-jsonld" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(PHYSICIAN)} />

  <h1>About Keegan Baldwin</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Keegan Baldwin is a movement-focused chiropractor on the Northern Beaches. He helps active people
    get out of pain and keep moving well for life, combining chiropractic care with a background in
    movement and mobility.
  </p>

  <h2>Why should you work with me?</h2>
  <p>
    Because I do not think one size fits all, and I can point at the results. All my methods have not
    only worked to improve my own movement, but many of my clients as well. You won’t find
    cookie-cutter approaches when working with me. Every body is different, therefore every client of
    mine gets unique advice and techniques to achieve the best results for them.
  </p>
  <p>
    I take a holistic approach to physical health using evidence-based techniques. That means helping
    you feel your best by targeting the root cause of your movement limitation and supporting you in
    building an efficient program to tackle it. If you are not happy with how your body feels or
    functions, I can help.
  </p>

  <h2>How did you get here?</h2>
  <p>
    Ten years of acrobatic training, then five years of study for a Bachelor’s and Master’s of
    Chiropractic Science. I’m Keegan, a passionate chiropractor with a heavy focus on mobility and
    functional movement, helping people transform their lives through better movement health.
  </p>
  <p>
    I have been involved with movement all my life. My childhood and teenage years set a solid
    foundation through team sports like soccer and hockey, as well as competitive swimming and Surf
    Life Saving. After leaving school, I dedicated 10 years of athletic training to mastering
    acrobatics. The pursuit of mastering my body’s movement led me to explore many adjacent
    disciplines, including handbalancing, yoga, flying trapeze, juggling, firespinning, and
    calisthenics.
  </p>
  <p>
    I developed a passion for movement and wanted to learn more and help others understand how to get
    the most out of their bodies. This led me to 5 years of study, resulting in a Bachelor’s and
    Master’s of Chiropractic Science. I graduated from Macquarie University in 2023. Now I leverage
    my knowledge of the body to help others master movement, whether that’s overcoming a painful
    injury or increasing their strength, flexibility, or coordination, supporting you in achieving
    and maintaining a healthy, balanced life.
  </p>

  <h2>Why does the handbalancing matter?</h2>
  <p>
    Because it is where the movement side of the practice comes from. Handstands demand strength,
    balance, mobility and body awareness at the same time, and training them for years is what taught
    me how those qualities depend on each other. That is the lens I bring to a patient who is stiff,
    sore, or stuck at a plateau.
  </p>
  <p>
    I still coach it, and I organise handstand meetups around the Northern Beaches.{' '}
    <a href="/services/handstand/">Handstand coaching and classes</a> have their own page if that is
    what brought you here.
  </p>

  <h2>Are you registered?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes. I am a chiropractor registered with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency,
    registration {business.ahpra}. Chiropractors in Australia complete a minimum five-year university
    degree covering anatomy, physiology, neurology, pathology, radiology and clinical practice, and
    are regulated by AHPRA, the same body that oversees doctors, physiotherapists and nurses.
  </p>

  <BookCta slug="new-patient" label="Book an appointment" showDetail={true} />

  <h2>Where to next?</h2>
  <ul class="next-links">
    <li><a href="/services/chiropractic-treatment/">Chiropractic &amp; Movement</a> — what a session actually involves</li>
    <li><a href="/contact/">Book an appointment</a></li>
    <li><a href="/services/">Services and pricing</a></li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }
  .next-links {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .next-links li {
    margin: 0.35rem 0;
  }
</style>
